  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest surface analysis shows a west to east oriented cold front\nmoving south through the St Lawrence Valley early this morning.\nLatest radar shows light showers along and ahead of the front.\nAs the front continues south these showers will move across the\nInternational Border and into the mountains before day break.\nWhile the front will wash out over the area later this morning,\nrecent runs of hi res guidance some light showers will hold\ntogether as they move south of the mountains towards the Maine\ncoast through mid morning. Shower chances diminish around mid\nday with skies turning mostly sunny during the afternoon as high\npressure rebuilds into the area from the north. Highs will range\nfrom the upper 60s north to low 80s south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low will start to deepen over northern Ontario early on \nWednesday, cross the Hudson Bay at night, and move into northern \nQuebec on Thursday. An associated surface low will drag a couple of \ncold fronts across the region Wednesday night through Thursday, \nleading to increasing chances of showers Wednesday afternoon. The \nbest chances for widespread rain will be Wednesday night (50 to 90 \npercent) with PWATs forecast to be anomalously high for this time of \nyear. Thus, there could be some locally heavy downpours but the \nheaviest showers should move through rather quickly Wednesday night. \nTrends continue to suggest that the best chances will be across \nnorthern New Hampshire and the western Maine mountains but almost \neverywhere will likely see at least some measurable rain. Given the \nprogressive nature of the system, it seems reasonable that totals \nwill mostly be in the 0.25 to 0.50 inch range, but some higher \namounts will be possible under any heavier showers that do move \noverhead. Unfortunately, it looks like southern New Hampshire and \nsouthwest Maine may once again miss out on the higher totals and \nwill likely see less. Rain chances will decrease early to mid \nThursday morning, but some showers may linger across the north \nthrough the afternoon.\n\nRegarding temperatures Wednesday: clouds will be on the increase, \nwhich should lead to cooler temperatures but increasing dewpoints. \nHighs are forecast to mainly range from the upper 60s and lower 70s \nnorth, to the low to mid 70s across western and coastal Maine, and \nthe mid to upper 70s over southern New Hampshire. A few lower 80s \nwill be possible near the Massachusetts border. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n \nWinds will really start to increase out of the west Thursday \nafternoon behind the secondary cold front after it sweeps through. \nStrong cold air advection and decent 3-hour pressure rises (2 to 4 \nmb) will provide favorable conditions for gusty winds to mix their \nway down to the surface. Depending on the location, the latest NAM \nsoundings suggest anywhere from 25 to 40 knots at the top of the \nmixed layer. Thus, we should see widespread gusts in the 25 to 35 \nmph range with some sporadic gusts up to 45 mph Thursday afternoon. \n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday will be warmer for those ahead of the \nsecondary front with some low to mid 80s possible across the \nsouthern half of New Hampshire and interior/coastal Maine. Thursday \nnight will see some cooler lows behind the front, but lingering \nbreezy winds and cloud cover will keep temperatures from bottoming \nout too much. Forecast lows range from the mid to upper 40s north, \nto the mid to upper 50s south and along the coast.\n\nFriday will be cooler, dry, and breezy with northwest winds gusting \nup to 30 mph or so through the first part of the day. Highs will \nmainly range from the low to mid 60s north, to the low to mid 70s \nsouth. Winds then relax under a clear sky Friday night which will \nlead to fantastic radiational cooling conditions. Expect widespread \nlows in the 40s with some 30s possible in the northern valleys.\n\nThe next trough and front will approach on Sunday, leading to our \nnext low chances of precipitation. Expect highs mainly in the 70s \nthrough the weekend and into the next work week with a couple of \nlower 80s across far southern New Hampshire.",
